@* MiniWorld -- the popover's own map figure (Task 15, READ-4/READ-5).
   design-direction.md: "The mini-map inside popovers (Task 15) inherits
   the same atlas-plate base naturally: a small engraved figure on a paper
   card." Read together with app.css's own pre-existing
   ".world-page .leaflet-tile-pane" comment ("scoped under .world-page so
   the (daylight) mini-map inside popovers is never affected"), this means:
   SAME NatGeo tile source map.js's init() already uses for the full map
   (the "atlas-plate base"), but WITHOUT the extra .world-page-scoped sepia
   warmth filter -- the popover itself is always a parchment/daylight
   artifact (design-direction.md's Concept section), so the map figure on
   it reads crisp, not muddied by a filter meant for the dusk page. This
   only holds as long as ExplorerPopover (and this component with it) is
   never rendered as a DOM descendant of .world-page -- both Reader.razor
   (Task 14) and World.razor (this task) render <ExplorerPopover> as a
   SIBLING of their page-root div, never nested inside it, so a mini-map
   opened from either page looks identical.

   CONTROLLER RULING (this task's dispatch): mini instances render markers
   ONLY -- no border overlay, no landmark labels, no arrows (320x240 is
   too small to read any of the three). This component only ever calls
   MapInterop.SetScene/FitScene -- never SetBorders/SetBordersVisible/
   SetLandmarks -- and map.js's own init(mini: true) path independently
   skips creating the border/arrow/landmark layers at all, and setScene's
   own arrow push (arrows travel bundled inside every Scene payload,
   unlike borders/landmarks which are separate calls this component simply
   never makes) is gated on the same mini flag -- belt and suspenders.

   Each MiniWorld instance owns exactly one MapInterop (and therefore one
   Leaflet map/DOM subtree); ExplorerPopover's own @if around <MiniWorld>
   (never rendering more than one at a time, and always via null in
   between -- see ExplorerPopover.razor's own comment) means Blazor tears
   this component down -- calling DisposeAsync below -- every time the
   mini-map area closes or a different popover node loads, so no Leaflet
   instance ever outlives the figure that created it.

   FIX ROUND 1: that last guarantee is not automatic just from DisposeAsync
   existing -- OnAfterRenderAsync's own awaits (MapInterop.Create, then
   Atlas.SceneScripture/SetScene/FitScene) are each a point where
   DisposeAsync can run concurrently and, without the _disposed flag below,
   would see _mapInterop still null mid-Create and no-op, letting the
   continuation that resolves afterward assign a MapInterop nothing would
   ever dispose -- a real leaked Leaflet instance. See _disposed's own
   comment and every check against it in OnAfterRenderAsync/DisposeAsync for
   how this is actually made true, not just stated. *@
@implements IAsyncDisposable
@implements IMapEvents
@inject AtlasClient Atlas
@inject IJSRuntime JS

<div class="popover-mini-map" data-testid="mini-map">
    <div class="mini-world-map" @ref="_mapEl"></div>
    <a class="mini-world-open-world" data-testid="mini-map-open-world"
       href="@($"/world?ref={Uri.EscapeDataString(SRef)}")">Open in world &rsaquo;</a>
</div>

@code {
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public string SRef { get; set; } = default!;

    private ElementReference _mapEl;
    private MapInterop? _mapInterop;

    // Fix round 1 finding: every await below is a point where DisposeAsync
    // can run concurrently (ExplorerPopover's own @if around <MiniWorld> --
    // see this component's header comment -- tears this component down the
    // instant the mini-map area closes or a different node loads, which can
    // land at ANY of these awaits if the user is fast, or a test is). Before
    // this flag existed, DisposeAsync running while MapInterop.Create's own
    // internal awaits (JS module import + Leaflet init) were still pending
    // saw _mapInterop still null and no-opped -- then Create's continuation
    // resolved and assigned a FRESH MapInterop to _mapInterop, which nothing
    // would ever read or dispose again: a real Leaflet map/DOM subtree
    // leaked, permanently, every time the race landed there. _disposed is
    // set FIRST thing in DisposeAsync (see below), then checked after EVERY
    // await in this method -- the moment it's true, whatever this method
    // was about to do next is pointless (the popover this map belongs to is
    // already gone), so it either disposes whatever it just created itself
    // (DisposeAsync can no longer do it -- it already ran) or simply bails,
    // never touching a _mapInterop DisposeAsync may have already disposed
    // and nulled out from underneath it.
    private bool _disposed;

    protected override async Task OnAfterRenderAsync(bool firstRender)
    {
        if (!firstRender)
        {
            return;
        }

        var interop = await MapInterop.Create(JS, _mapEl, mini: true, this);
        if (_disposed)
        {
            // DisposeAsync ran while the await above was pending -- at that
            // moment _mapInterop was still null, so its own cleanup (below)
            // had nothing to dispose. `interop` is now the ONLY reference to
            // the real Leaflet map/DOM subtree map.js's own `instances` Map
            // is still holding (see map.js's `destroy`) -- dispose it
            // directly here rather than assigning it to _mapInterop, which
            // nothing would ever read or dispose again.
            await interop.DisposeAsync();
            return;
        }

        _mapInterop = interop;

        try
        {
            var scene = await Atlas.SceneScripture(SRef);
            if (_disposed)
            {
                // DisposeAsync ran during this fetch. _mapInterop was
                // already assigned by the time it ran, so DisposeAsync's
                // own cleanup already disposed it (and nulled the field) --
                // nothing left to do here.
                return;
            }

            await _mapInterop.SetScene(scene);
            if (_disposed)
            {
                return;
            }

            await _mapInterop.FitScene();
        }
        catch (Exception)
        {
            // A mini-map that fails to load is a quiet nicety gone missing,
            // not a popover-breaking error -- the surrounding popover's own
            // body/chips already loaded successfully by the time this runs.
            // The 320x240 figure just stays an empty (tile-only) map. (A
            // disposed _mapInterop mid-fetch never reaches a call that could
            // throw here -- both _disposed checks above return first.)
        }
    }

    // IMapEvents: mini-map markers stay non-interactive beyond whatever
    // Leaflet gives for free (a pointer cursor on hover) -- popovers-from-
    // mini-maps are explicitly NOT required by this task's brief, so every
    // callback here is a deliberate no-op, not a missing feature. map.js's
    // wireEvents still fires these for every marker regardless of mini
    // (same code path as the full map), so "marker hover" stays trivially
    // available per the brief -- it just resolves to nothing happening.
    public void OnPlaceHover(string id, double x, double y)
    {
    }

    public void OnPlaceLeave()
    {
    }

    public void OnPlaceClick(string id, double x, double y)
    {
    }

    public void OnArrowHover(string key, double x, double y)
    {
    }

    public void OnArrowLeave()
    {
    }

    public void OnArrowClick(string key, double x, double y)
    {
    }

    public async ValueTask DisposeAsync()
    {
        // Set FIRST, before any await -- this is the flag OnAfterRenderAsync
        // checks after each of its own awaits (see its comment). Single-
        // threaded Blazor WASM guarantees no other continuation can run
        // between this assignment and the field capture/null below, so by
        // the time ANY interleaved OnAfterRenderAsync continuation resumes
        // and reads _disposed, it is already true.
        _disposed = true;

        // Capture-then-null rather than a plain null check: if
        // OnAfterRenderAsync's own Create() await is still pending right
        // now, _mapInterop is still null here too -- correctly nothing to
        // dispose FROM THIS METHOD (OnAfterRenderAsync's own _disposed
        // check, once its await resolves, disposes that interop itself
        // instead). If _mapInterop IS already assigned, nulling it here
        // (before awaiting its disposal) means OnAfterRenderAsync's later
        // _disposed checks never touch an interop this method has already
        // started disposing.
        var interop = _mapInterop;
        _mapInterop = null;
        if (interop is not null)
        {
            await interop.DisposeAsync();
        }
    }
}
